        "problem": "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=2y-x?$",
        "worked_solution": "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\u2207f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(2y-x) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(2y-x) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\u2207f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(-2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ \u2207f(2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ \u2207f(2,-2) & =-i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png)",
